      "problem": "Find the minimum value of $y$ on the graph $y=|x-3|+3.$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that $y=|x-h|+k$ is a vertical translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $h$ units right and $k$ units up.\n\nThe function $y=|x-3|+3$ has $h=3$ and $k=3.$ Therefore, it is a translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $3$ units *right* and $3$ units *up*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250789/assets/d5c67a156d80c6dabd832539a4912c2bdd7ef38af9735fcade7ee46e4ba66eaa.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the minimum value of $y$ is $3.$ This occurs at the point $(3,3).$",
